== Chapter XIV

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[#emph[The general use of the foregoing Directions. The great Direction for the accomplishment of the Work aimed at. Act Faith on Christ; The several Ways whereby this may be done. Consideration of the Fulness in Christ for Relief, proposed. Great Expectations from Christ: Grounds of these Expectations. His Mercifulness, his Faithfulness. Event of such Expectations: On the part of Christ: On the part of Believers. Faith peculiarly to be acted on the Death of Christ:] Rom 6:3, 4, 5, 6. #emph[The Work of the Spirit in this whole business.]]
]

#smallcaps[NOW] the Considerations which I have hitherto insisted on, are rather of things #emph[preparatory] to the work aimed at, than such as will #emph[effect] it. It is the #emph[hearts] due #emph[preparation] for the work itself, without which it will not be accomplished, that hitherto I have aimed at.

Directions for the work itself are very few; I mean that are peculiar to it. And they are these that follow.

==== The Work Itself: Act Faith on Christ

First, Set Faith at work on Christ for the #emph[killing] of thy sin. His #emph[blood] is the great sovereign remedy for sin-sick souls. Live in this and thou wilt die a Conqueror. Yea thou wilt through the good #emph[providence] of God, live to see thy lust dead at thy feet. But thou wilt say, how shall Faith act itself on Christ for this end and purpose. I say sundry ways.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. By faith fill thy soul with a due consideration of that #emph[provision] which is laid up in Jesus Christ for this end and purpose, that all thy #emph[lusts,] this very lust wherewith thou art entangled, may be mortified by Faith; ponder on this, that though thou art no way able in or by thyself to get the conquest over thy #emph[distemper,] though thou art even weary of contending, and art utterly ready to faint, Luke 15:17 yet that there is enough in Jesus Christ, to yield thee relief Phil 4:13. It staid the #emph[prodigal] when he was ready to faint, that #emph[yet] there was #emph[bread] enough in his #emph[father's house]\; Though he was at a distance from it, yet it #emph[relieved] him, and staid him, that there it was. In thy greatest #emph[distress] and anguish, consider that #emph[fullness] of Grace, those #emph[Riches,] those #emph[Treasures] of strength, #emph[might] and #emph[help,] that, are laid up in him, for our support,#footnote[Isa 40:28, 29, 30, 31.] John 1:16. Col 1:19. Let them come into, and abide in thy mind. Consider that he is #emph[exalted] and made a #emph[Prince] and a #emph[Savior] to give repentance unto #emph[Israel,] Acts 5:31 and if to give #emph[Repentance,] to give #emph[Mortification,] without which the other is not, nor can be, Christ tells us that we obtain #emph[purging] Grace by abiding in him, John 15:3. To act faith upon the fulness that is in Christ for our #emph[supply,] is an eminent #emph[way] of #emph[abiding] in Christ, for both our insition and abode is by Faith, Rom 11:19, 20. Let then thy soul by faith be exercised with such thoughts and apprehensions as these. I am a poor weak Creature; unstable as water, I cannot excel. This corruption is too #emph[hard] for me, and is at the very door of ruining my soul: and #emph[what to do] I know not: My soul is become as #emph[parched] ground and an #emph[habitation] of Dragons; I have made promises and #emph[broken them; vows] and engagements have been as a thing of #emph[nought]\; many #emph[persuasions] have I had, that I had got the #emph[victory] and should be #emph[delivered,] but I am deceived; so that I plainly see, that without some #emph[eminent] succor and assistance, I am lost, and shall be prevailed on, to an utter #emph[relinquishment] of God; but yet though this be my state and Condition, Yet let the hands that hang down be lifted up,#footnote[John 1:16. Matt 28:18.] and the #emph[feeble knees] be strengthened; behold the Lord Christ that hath all #emph[fullness] of Grace in his heart, all #emph[fullness] of power in his hand; He is able to #emph[slay] all these his enemies. There is sufficient #emph[provision] in him for my relief and assistance: He can take my drooping dying soul, and make me more than a #emph[Conqueror.] #footnote[Rom 8:37.] #emph[Why sayest thou O my soul my way is hid from the Lord and my Judgment is passed over from my God? Hast thou not known, hast thou not heard that the everlasting God, the Lord, the Creator of the ends of the earth fainteth not, neither is weary; there is no searching of his understanding; he giveth power to the faint, and to them that have no might he increaseth strength. Even the youths shall faint and be weary, and the young men shall utterly fail; but they that wait upon the Lord shall renew their strength, they shall mount up with wings as Eagles, they shall run and not be weary, they shall walk and not faint,] Isa 40:27–31. He can make the dry parched ground of my soul to become a #emph[pool,] and my thirsty barren heart as springs of water: yea he can make this #emph[habitation] of #emph[Dragons,] this heart so full of abominable lusts, and fiery temptations, to be a place for #emph[Grass] and #emph[fruit] to himself. Isa 35. So God stayed #emph[Paul] under his #emph[Temptation,] with the Consideration of the #emph[sufficiency] of his Grace: #emph[My Grace is sufficient for thee,] 2 Cor 12:9. Though he were not immediately so far made #emph[partaker] of it as to be freed from his #emph[Temptation,] yet the #emph[sufficiency] of it in God, for that end and purpose, was enough to stay his Spirit. I say then, by faith be much in the #emph[Consideration] of that supply and the fullness of it, that is in Jesus Christ; and how he can at any time give thee strength and deliverance. Now if hereby thou dost not find #emph[success] to a conquest, yet thou wilt be #emph[staid] in the #emph[Chariot,] that thou shalt not fly out of the #emph[field] until the battle be ended; Thou wilt be kept from an utter #emph[despondency,] and a lying down under thy unbelief; or a turning aside to #emph[false] means and remedies that in the issue will not relieve thee. The efficacy of this #emph[Consideration] will be found only in the practice.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. #emph[Raise] up thy heart by #emph[faith] to an #emph[expectation] of #emph[Relief] from Christ; #emph[Relief] in this case from Christ is like the #emph[Prophets] vision, Hab 2:3. It is for an #emph[appointed] time; but at the end it shall speak, and not lie; though it #emph[tarry,] yet wait for it, because it #emph[will surely] come, it will not tarry; though it may seem somewhat long to thee, whilst thou art under thy trouble and perplexity, yet it shall #emph[surely] come in the appointed time of the Lord Jesus, which is the #emph[best] season. If then thou canst #emph[raise] up thy heart to a #emph[settled Expectation] of relief from Jesus Christ; if thine eyes are towards him, as the eyes of a #emph[servant to the hand of his master,] Ps 123:2 when he expects to receive somewhat from him, thy soul shall be #emph[satisfied,] Isa 7:8 he will assuredly #emph[deliver] thee; He will #emph[slay] the lust, and thy latter end shall be peace; #emph[only] look for it, at His hand expect when and how He will do it. #emph[If you will not believe, surely ye shall not be established.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Q.] But thou wilt say, what #emph[ground] have I to #emph[build] such an #emph[Expectation] upon; so that I may expect not to be deceived?]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[A.] As thou hast necessity to put thee on this course, John 6:68, thou must be #emph[relieved] and saved this way or none; to whom wilt thou go? So there are in the #emph[Lord Jesus] innumerable #emph[things] to encourage and engage thee to this #emph[Expectation.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[For the necessity of it, I have in part discovered it before, when I manifested that this is the work of #emph[Faith,] and of #emph[Believers] only. #emph[Without me] (says Christ) #emph[you can do nothing,] John 15:5 speaking with especial relation to the purging of the heart from sin, v. 2. Mortification of any sin, must be by a supply of Grace. Of ourselves we cannot do it. Now it hath pleased the Father that in Christ all #emph[fullness should dwell,] Col 1:19 that of his fulness we might receive #emph[Grace for Grace,] John 1:16. He is the head, from whence the #emph[new man must] have influences of life and strength, or it will decay every day. If we are strengthened with #emph[might in the inner man,] Col 1:11, it is by Christs #emph[dwelling] in our hearts by faith, Eph 3:16, 17. That this work is not to be done without the #emph[Spirit,] I have also showed before. Whence then do we #emph[expect] the Spirit? From whom do we look for him? Who hath promised him to us, having procured him for us? Ought not all our expectations to this #emph[purpose] to be on Christ alone? Let this then be #emph[fixed] upon thy heart, that if thou hast not #emph[relief] from him, thou shalt never have any: All ways, endeavors, contendings, that are not #emph[animated] by this expectation of relief from Christ and him only, are to no purpose, will do thee no Good: yea if they are any thing but supportments of thy heart in this expectation, or means appointed by himself, for the receiving help from him, they are in vain.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Now farther to engage thee to this Expectation.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. Consider his #emph[mercifulness,] tenderness, and #emph[kindness,] as he is our great #emph[high Priest,] at the #emph[right hand] of God. Assuredly he #emph[pities] thee in thy distress; saith He, as one #emph[Whom his Mother comforteth so will I comfort you,] Isa 66:13. He hath the #emph[tenderness] of a #emph[Mother] to a sucking Child, Heb 2:17, 18. #emph[Wherefore in all things it behoved him to be made like unto his brethren, that he might be a merciful and faithful high Priest in things pertaining to God, to make reconciliation for the sins of the People, for in that himself hath suffered being tempted, he is able to succor them that are tempted:] How is the ability of Christ upon the account of his suffering proposed to us? #emph[in that he himself hath suffered being tempted, he is able.] Did the sufferings and Temptations of Christ add to his ability and power? Not doubtless considered absolutely and in itself: But the #emph[Ability] here mentioned, is such as hath #emph[readiness,] proneness, #emph[willingness,] to put itself forth accompanying of it; it is an Ability of will against all dissuasions; He is #emph[able] having #emph[suffered] and being #emph[tempted,] to break through all #emph[dissuasions] to the contrary, to relieve poor tempted souls: δύναται βοηθῆσαι, He is #emph[able] to help. It is a metonymy of the Effect. For he can now be moved to help having been so tempted. So ch. 4:15, 16. #emph[For we have not an high Priest which cannot be touched with the feeling of our infirmities, but was in all points tempted like as we are, yet without sin. Let us therefore come boldly to the Throne of Grace, that we may obtain Mercy, and find Grace to help in time of need.] The Exhortation of v. 16 is the #emph[same] that I am upon, namely, that we would entertain Expectations of Relief from Christ, which the Apostle there calls χάριν εἰς εὔκαιρον βοήθειαν, #emph[Grace for seasonable help]\; if ever (says the Soul) #emph[help] were #emph[seasonable,] it would be so to me in my #emph[present] Condition. This is that which I long for, Grace for #emph[seasonable help.] I am ready to die, to perish, to be lost for ever; Iniquity will prevail against me, if #emph[help] come not in; says the #emph[Apostle,] Expect this Help, this #emph[Relief,] this Grace from Christ; yea, but on what account? That he lays down v. 15 and we may observe, that the word v. 16 which we have translated to #emph[obtain,] is, λάβωμεν, ἵνα λάβωμεν ἔλεον, that we may #emph[receive] it, #emph[suitable] and #emph[seasonable] help will come in. I shall freely say this one thing of #emph[establishing] the Soul by Faith in expectation of #emph[Relief] from Jesus Christ,#footnote[Matt 11:28.] on the account of his #emph[Mercifulness] as our High-Priest will be more available to the #emph[ruin] of thy Lust and Distemper, and have a #emph[better] and #emph[speedier] issue than all the #emph[rigidest] Means of #emph[self-maceration,] that ever any of the #emph[sons] of men engaged themselves into. Yea let me add, that never any Soul did or shall perish by the power of any #emph[lust,] #footnote[Isa 55:1, 2, 3. Rev 3:18.] #emph[sin] or #emph[corruption,] who could raise his #emph[soul] by Faith to an #emph[expectation] of #emph[relief] from Jesus Christ.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. Consider his #emph[Faithfulness] who hath promised, which may raise thee up, and #emph[confirm] thee in this #emph[waiting] in an Expectation of Relief. He hath promised to relieve in such Cases, and he will fulfill his word to the utmost. God tells us that his #emph[Covenant] with us is like the Ordinances of #emph[Heaven,] the #emph[Sun, Moon] and #emph[Stars,] which have their certain Courses, Jer 31:36. Thence #emph[David] said, that he #emph[watched] for Relief from God, as one #emph[watcheth] for the #emph[Morning,] a thing that will certainly come in its appointed season; so will be thy Relief from Christ. It will come in its season, as the #emph[dew] and #emph[rain] upon the parched Ground; for Faithful is he who hath promised. Particular Promises to this purpose are #emph[innumerable]\; with some of them (that seem peculiarly to suit to his Condition) let the Soul be always furnished.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Now there are two eminent #emph[Advantages] which always attend this Expectation of succor from Jesus Christ.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. It engages him to a full and speedy Assistance; nothing doth more engage the Heart of a man to be useful and helpful to another, than his Expectation of help from him, if justly raised and #emph[countenanced] by him who is to give the Relief. Our #emph[Lord Jesus] hath raised our Hearts by his #emph[kindness,] Care and #emph[Promises,] to this Expectation; Certainly our rising up unto it, must needs be a great Engagement upon him to assist us accordingly. This the #emph[Psalmist] gives us as an approved #emph[Maxim, Thou Lord never forsakest them that put their trust in thee.] When the Heart is once won to rest in God, to repose himself on him, He will assuredly #emph[satisfy] it. He will never be as water that fails, nor hath he said at any time to the #emph[seed of Jacob, Seek ye my face in vain.] If Christ be chosen for the Foundation of our Supply, he will not fail us.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. It engages the Heart to attend #emph[diligently] to all #emph[ways] and Means whereby Christ is wont to #emph[communicate] himself to the Soul, and so takes in the real Assistance of all Graces and Ordinances whatever. He that expects any thing from a man, applies himself to the ways and Means whereby it may be obtained. The Beggar that expects an Alms, lies at his #emph[door] or in his way, from whom he doth expect it. The way whereby, and the Means wherein Christ communicates himself is, and are, his Ordinances ordinarily. He that expects any thing from him, must attend upon him therein. It is the #emph[expectation] of Faith that sets the Heart on work. 'Tis not an idle groundless Hope that I speak of. If now there be any Vigor, Efficacy and Power in #emph[Prayer] or #emph[Sacraments] to this End of #emph[mortifying] Sin, a man will assuredly be interested in it all, by this Expectation of Relief from Christ. On this account I reduce all particular actings, by Prayer, Meditation and the like, to this Head; and so shall not farther insist on them. When they are #emph[grounded] on this bottom, and spring from this #emph[Root,] they are of singular use to this purpose; and not else.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Now on this Direction for the Mortification of a prevailing Distemper you may have a thousand #emph[probatum est's]\; Who hath walked with God under this #emph[Temptation,] and hath not found the use and success of it? I dare leave the Soul under it, without adding any more. Only some particulars relating thereunto may be mentioned.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[(1) Act Faith #emph[peculiarly] upon the #emph[Death,] Blood and Cross of Christ; that is, on Christ as #emph[crucified] and slain. #emph[Mortification] of Sin is peculiarly from the #emph[Death] of Christ. It is one peculiar, yea eminent End of the #emph[Death] of Christ, which shall assuredly be accomplished by it. He died to destroy the works of the Devil; whatever came upon our #emph[Natures] by his first Temptation, whatever receives strength in our Persons by his daily suggestions, Christ died to destroy it all. #emph[He gave himself for us, that he might redeem us from all iniquity, and purify unto himself a peculiar people, zealous of good works,] Titus 2:14. This was his Aim and Intendment (wherein he will not fail) in his #emph[giving himself] for us. That we might be freed from the Power of our Sins, and purified from all our defiling Lusts, was his Design. #emph[He gave himself for the Church, that he might sanctify and cleanse it, that he might present it to himself a glorious Church, not having spot or wrinkle, or any such thing, but that it should be holy and without blemish,] Eph 5:25, 26, 27. And this by virtue of his #emph[death,] in various and several degrees shall be accomplished. Hence our washing, purging and cleansing is every where ascribed to his Blood, 1 John 1:7. Heb 1:3. Rev 1:5. That being sprinkled on us, #emph[Purges our Consciences from dead works to serve the living God,] Heb 9:14. This is that we aim at, this we are in pursuit of; that our Consciences may be #emph[purged from dead works]\; that they may be rooted out, destroyed, and have place in us no more. This shall certainly be brought about by the #emph[Death] of Christ: There will virtue go out from thence to this purpose. Indeed, all Supplies of the Spirit,#footnote[Communion with Christ, #emph[ch. 7, 8.]] all Communications of Grace and Power, are from hence, as I have elsewhere showed. Thus the Apostle states it; Rom 6:2 is the Case proposed that we have in hand; #emph[How shall we that are dead unto sin live any longer therein?] Dead to Sin by #emph[Profession]\; dead to Sin by Obligation to be so; dead, to Sin by a Participation of Virtue and Power for the Killing of it; dead to Sin by #emph[Union] and #emph[Interest] in Christ, in and by whom it is killed: How shall we live therein? This he presses by sundry Considerations, all taken from the Death of Christ, in the ensuing Verses. This must not be, v. 3. #emph[Know you not that so many of us as were baptized into Jesus Christ were baptized into his Death?] We have in #emph[Baptism] an Evidence of our Implantation into Christ; we are #emph[baptized] into him; But what of him are we baptized into an Interest in? His #emph[Death,] saith he: If indeed we are #emph[baptized] into Christ, and beyond Outward Profession, we are baptized into his #emph[Death.] The Explication of this, of our being #emph[baptized into the Death of Christ,] the Apostle gives us, vv. 4, 5. #emph[Therefore we are buried with him by Baptism into Death, that like as Christ was raised up from the dead by the Glory of the Father, even so we also should walk in Newness of life; Knowing this, that our Old man is crucified with him, that the body of sin might be destroyed, that henceforth we should not serve sin.] This is (saith he) our #emph[being baptized into the Death of Christ,] namely, our Conformity thereunto. To be #emph[dead] unto Sin, to have our #emph[corruptions] mortified, as he was put to #emph[death] for Sin; so that as he was raised up to #emph[Glory,] we may be raised up to #emph[Grace] and Newness of Life.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[He tells us whence it is that we have this #emph[Baptism] into the Death of Christ, v. 6 and this is from the #emph[Death] of Christ itself: Our old Man is #emph[crucified with him,] that the Body of Sin might be destroyed: συνεσταυρώθη, is #emph[crucified] with him; not in #emph[respect] of Time, but of #emph[Causality]\; we are #emph[crucified] with him, #emph[meritoriously] in that he procured the #emph[Spirit] for us, to #emph[mortify] Sin; #emph[efficiently,] in that from his #emph[Death virtue] comes forth for our #emph[crucifying]\; in the way of a #emph[Representation] and #emph[Exemplar,] we shall assuredly be #emph[crucified] unto Sin, as he was for #emph[our] Sin. This is that the Apostle intends. Christ #emph[by his Death] destroying the works of the Devil, #emph[procuring] the Spirit for us, hath so killed #emph[Sin] as to its Reign in Believers, that it shall not obtain its #emph[End] and Dominion.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[(2) Then act #emph[Faith] on the #emph[Death] of Christ, and that under these two #emph[Notions]:#footnote[Phil 3:10. Col 3:3. 1 Pet 1:18.]]]

- 1\. In expectation of #emph[Power.]
- 2\. In endeavors for #emph[Conformity.]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[For the First, the Direction given in #emph[general] may suffice.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[As to the latter, that of the Apostle may give us some Light into our Direction, Gal 3:1. Let Faith look on Christ in the Gospel as he is set forth #emph[dying] and #emph[crucified] for us:#footnote[1 Cor 15:31. 1 Pet 1:16. 1 Pet 5:1, 2. Col 1:3.] Look on him #emph[under] the weight of our #emph[Sins, praying, bleeding, dying]: bring him in that Condition into thy #emph[heart] by Faith: apply his #emph[blood] so shed to thy Corruptions: do this daily. I might draw out this Consideration to a great length, in sundry particulars, but I must come to a close.]]

==== The Work of the Spirit

I have only then to add, the Heads of the work of the Spirit in this business of #emph[Mortification,] which is so peculiarly ascribed to him.

In one word: This #emph[whole work] which I have described as our Duty, is effected, carried on, and accomplished by the Power of the Spirit, in all the parts and degrees of it: As,

1\. He alone #emph[clearly] and fully #emph[convinces] the Heart of the #emph[evil] and #emph[guilt] and danger of the Corruption, Lust, or Sin to be Mortified. Without this Conviction (or whilst it is faint, that the Heart can wrestle with it, or digest it, there will be no thorough-work made. An #emph[unbelieving] Heart (as in part we have all such) will shift with any Consideration, until it be #emph[over-powered] by clear and evident #emph[Convictions:] Now this is the #emph[proper work] of the Spirit: He #emph[convinces of Sin,] John 16:8. He alone can do it; If men's #emph[rational] Considerations, with the preaching of the #emph[letter,] were able to #emph[convince] them of Sin, we should (it may be) see more #emph[Convictions] than we do. There comes by the preaching of the Word an #emph[Apprehension] upon the #emph[understandings] of men, that they are #emph[Sinners,] that #emph[such] and #emph[such] things are Sins; that themselves are guilty of them; But this #emph[light] is not powerful, nor doth it #emph[lay hold] on the #emph[practical] Principles of the Soul, so as to conform the Mind and Will unto them, to produce Effects suitable to such an #emph[Apprehension.] And therefore it is, that wise and knowing men, destitute of the #emph[Spirit,] do not think those things to be Sins at all wherein the #emph[chief] Movings and Actings of #emph[Lust] do consist. It is the Spirit alone that can do, that doth this work to the purpose. And this is the first thing that the Spirit doth in order to the #emph[Mortification] of any Lust whatever; It convinces the Soul of all the #emph[evil] of it, cuts off all its #emph[pleas,] discovers all its #emph[deceits,] stops all its Evasions, answers its Pretenses, makes the Soul own its Abomination, and lie down under the sense of it. Unless this be done, all that follows is in vain.

2\. The Spirit alone reveals unto us the #emph[Fulness] of Christ for our Relief, which is the Consideration that stays the Heart from false Ways, and from despairing Despondency, 1 Cor 2:8.

3\. The Spirit alone #emph[establishes] the Heart in expectation of Relief from Christ; which is the great #emph[sovereign] Means of Mortification, as hath been discovered, 2 Cor 1:21.

4\. The Spirit alone brings the #emph[Cross] of Christ into our Hearts, with its #emph[Sin-killing] Power; for by the Spirit are we #emph[baptized] into the Death of Christ.

5\. The Spirit is the #emph[Author] and Finisher of our #emph[Sanctification]\; gives new Supplies and Influences of Grace for #emph[Holiness] and Sanctification, when the contrary Principle is weakened and abated, Eph 3:16, 17, 18.

6\. In all the Souls Addresses to God in this Condition, it hath #emph[Supportment] from the Spirit. Whence is the #emph[power, life] and #emph[vigor] of Prayer? Whence its Efficacy to prevail with God? Is it not from the Spirit? He is the Spirit of #emph[supplication] promised to them who look on him whom they have pierced, Zech 12:10 enabling them to pray with #emph[sighs] and #emph[groans] that cannot be uttered, Rom 8:26. This is confessed to be the great #emph[Medium] or way of Faiths #emph[prevailing] with God. Thus #emph[Paul] dealt with his Temptation, whatever it were; #emph[I besought God that it might depart from me,] 2 Cor 12:8. What is the work of the Spirit in Prayer, whence, and how it gives us in #emph[assistance,] and makes us to #emph[prevail,] what we are to do that we may enjoy his Help for that purpose, is not my present Intendment to demonstrate.
